---
// /docs/install — the plugin install and its one requirement.
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/docs-layout/DocsLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../../lib/base.ts";
---

<DocsLayout
  current="install"
  title="Install"
  description="Install Agentry as a Claude Code plugin in two commands, then set it up with /agentry:init. OpenCode is supported in 0.4.0. Node ≥ 24."
  eyebrow="Getting Started · Install"
  lede="Two commands install the plugin; a third sets it up for your repo. That is all you need to start."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 class="prose__h">Install the plugin</h2>
    <p>
      Agentry installs as a Claude Code plugin from its GitHub repo. From a Claude Code session in your repo, add it, install it, and let <code>/agentry:init</code> draft its process file from what it finds:
    </p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/plugin marketplace add silentthreadstudio/agentry#v0.4.2
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/plugin install agentry@agentry-dev
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/agentry:init</code></pre>
    <p>
      That's the whole setup. The conducting front door (<code>/agentry:go</code>), the SDLC
      specialists, and the durable memory layer all run from here — entirely from the terminal,
      no account and no build step. Head to <a href={withBase("docs/quickstart/")}>Quickstart</a>
      to hand it a first task.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <code>#v0.4.2</code> suffix pins the install to the 0.4.2 release; the repo's main branch moves with
      development. To move to a later release, remove the marketplace and add it again with the new tag. Each
      release is also on <a href="https://github.com/silentthreadstudio/agentry/releases">GitHub Releases</a>
      as a zip.
    </p>

    <h2 class="prose__h">OpenCode</h2>
    <p>
      OpenCode is supported in 0.4.0. The package, <code>agentry-opencode</code>, is on npm under <code>latest</code>,
      so no tag is needed. It needs OpenCode V2. Add the plugin,
      then restart the OpenCode service so it loads:
    </p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode plugin add agentry-opencode
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode service restart
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>npx agentry-opencode init --host opencode --vendor --apply</code></pre>
    <p>
      The plugin registers <code>agentry_board</code>, <code>agentry_lane</code> and <code>agentry_mem</code> as
      native tools. The init command gives a repo the OpenCode payload (agents, commands and skills generated from
      the plugin). Without <code>--apply</code> it previews. <code>--vendor</code> copies the CLI into the repo's <code>.agentry/</code>,
      so the agents' CLI fallback works without a checkout.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a repo with no learned process the AGENTS.md block is skipped, and the command says so. In OpenCode, run
      <code>/agentry/init</code> to learn your board, then run the init again to add the AGENTS.md block.
    </p>

    <h3 class="prose__h">Install globally</h3>
    <p>
      One copy of the payload for every repo, in OpenCode's global config dir
      (<code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/opencode</code>, else <code>~/.config/opencode</code>):
    </p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>npm install -g agentry-opencode
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode plugin add agentry-opencode
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode service restart
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>agentry init --host opencode --global --apply</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then, per repo, run <code>/agentry/init</code> in OpenCode to learn the board, and
      <code>agentry init --host opencode --apply</code> for the repo's AGENTS.md block. Run that repo init without
      <code>--vendor</code>; its payload overrides the global one. <code>--global</code> never touches a repo and never
      removes your own files there.
    </p>
    <p>
      Per project pins the version in each repo and is shared through <code>.opencode/</code>; global is one copy for all repos.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <span class="note__key">Requires</span>
      <span>Node ≥ 24 — the memory engine uses Node's built-in <code>node:sqlite</code>, so there are no native modules to compile.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="prose__cta">
      <p>
        Next: <a href={withBase("docs/permissions/")}>Permissions</a> covers how a tool call gets
        approved.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
